WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.450 --> 00:00:09.879
So I’ve brought you to the Royal Society
of Chemistry. This is the professional society

00:00:09.879 --> 00:00:15.959
for chemists in the UK and some other countries.
And we’ve come here because I want to see

00:00:15.959 --> 00:00:21.180
the Presidential Badge of Office. I’ve heard
about it lots of times but never actually

00:00:21.180 --> 00:00:26.060
seen it and it’s meant to have all sorts
of interesting elements in the badge itself.

00:00:26.060 --> 00:00:28.390
So let’s go inside and see if we can see
it.

00:00:28.390 --> 00:00:42.090
I’ve never been down these stairs before.
It’s like Alice in Wonderland through the

00:00:42.090 --> 00:00:49.070
little door.
So we’re now in the vault, never been here

00:00:49.070 --> 00:00:55.370
it’s quite, quite unusual. It’s a tiny
room I can hardly move in here. And so I’ve

00:00:55.370 --> 00:01:04.420
been shown where the Presidential Badge is,
so let’s get it out.

00:01:04.420 --> 00:01:13.200
So here it is, let’s see what’s inside
the box.

00:01:13.200 --> 00:01:20.329
So this is the badge. The ribbon is quite
interesting because the ribbon itself is dyed

00:01:20.329 --> 00:01:26.210
with a sample of mauveine, which was the first
artificial dye that had ever been made, was

00:01:26.210 --> 00:01:33.899
made by Perkins, discovered it when he was
18, was a millionaire by the time he was 30,

00:01:33.899 --> 00:01:39.249
a good role model for chemists nowadays. And
it was the first dye that had ever been made

00:01:39.249 --> 00:01:44.670
that was purple. Suddenly purple became really
fashionable, everybody was wearing purple

00:01:44.670 --> 00:01:51.249
clothes. And this was in the mid-nineteenth
century and was really the start of the chemical

00:01:51.249 --> 00:01:57.369
industry in the United Kingdom. So we’ll
turn our attention now to the badge. So this

00:01:57.369 --> 00:02:02.939
is the badge that is worn by the President
of the Royal Society of Chemistry on official

00:02:02.939 --> 00:02:09.780
occasions when he is being the President.
Here in the middle we have Priestley, and

00:02:09.780 --> 00:02:17.780
Priestley is standing inside a hexagon which
is meant to represent benzene. Really in benzene

00:02:17.780 --> 00:02:23.170
all the sides should be exactly the same length,
but here you can see it has been distorted

00:02:23.170 --> 00:02:28.530
a bit in this direction because Priestley
is taller than he is fat.

00:02:28.530 --> 00:02:34.700
Priestley was the discoverer of oxygen, although
there is some argument that the chemist Scheele

00:02:34.700 --> 00:02:41.159
might have discovered it at the same time.
This rim here, like the rim of the cartwheel,

00:02:41.159 --> 00:02:48.870
is made out of gold and you can see it, it’s
a nice golden colour. And then all of these

00:02:48.870 --> 00:02:58.250
spokes are made from metals and each spoke
is made from a different metal. But the first

00:02:58.250 --> 00:03:03.359
thing you can see is that they all look pretty
much the same because if a metal isn’t coloured

00:03:03.359 --> 00:03:10.659
like gold they all look shiny and a silvery
colour, and so unless you’re a specialist

00:03:10.659 --> 00:03:13.469
you probably can’t tell the difference between
them.

00:03:13.469 --> 00:03:18.760
Are there people who could look at those metals
then, without doing any chemistry on them,

00:03:18.760 --> 00:03:24.690
and tell me what they were made from?
There’s probably some, most people would

00:03:24.690 --> 00:03:29.560
be able to tell the difference if they started
lifting them up because some are very much

00:03:29.560 --> 00:03:35.760
denser than others. Tungsten for example would
be much denser than zinc, and so if you could

00:03:35.760 --> 00:03:41.209
actually feel them individually they would
be much easier to tell the difference.

00:03:41.209 --> 00:03:47.980
So this one here is zirconium, which is quite
a light metal. This is the one that is used

00:03:47.980 --> 00:03:56.230
for cladding and nuclear power plants. And
next to that is cobalt which is a metal that

00:03:56.230 --> 00:04:01.079
is used quite widely in catalysts, it’s
also used for some sorts of magnets that are

00:04:01.079 --> 00:04:08.061
very powerful. Then after that, I can’t
read what this one is, I think this one is

00:04:08.061 --> 00:04:14.849
platinum here which is one of the very valuable
metals, it’s not the most valuable, but

00:04:14.849 --> 00:04:22.630
one of the most valuable. This one here is
nickel which again is used in some catalysts.

00:04:22.630 --> 00:04:29.850
Very finely divided nickel is used for hydrogenation
catalysts, adding hydrogen to organic compounds.

00:04:29.850 --> 00:04:34.560
And nickel is also interesting because it
was the first metal that was discovered to

00:04:34.560 --> 00:04:41.890
react with carbon monoxide to make a volatile
compound, it boils at room temperature. Think

00:04:41.890 --> 00:04:50.210
of that, a metal compound that’s a gas if
you heat it just above 25°C. Then going round

00:04:50.210 --> 00:04:59.820
here we have titanium. Titanium is a very
light metal and very strong. And next to that

00:04:59.820 --> 00:05:09.670
one is iridium, which is another metal similar
to platinum and rhodium, it is very expensive,

00:05:09.670 --> 00:05:17.080
it is used in some catalysts. It’s used
in the catalyst to make ethanoic acid or acetic

00:05:17.080 --> 00:05:22.250
acid, one of the components of vinegar. On
an industrial scale iridium makes a really

00:05:22.250 --> 00:05:32.040
good catalyst for this. Here we have niobium
which is used, I’m not sure what niobium

00:05:32.040 --> 00:05:37.840
is used for, I think it can be used to reinforce
steel and it is not nearly as important a

00:05:37.840 --> 00:05:47.990
metal as some of the others. And then going
round here we have tungsten. Tungsten is the

00:05:47.990 --> 00:05:54.160
highest melting point metal there is. The
one down here is palladium, it’s lighter

00:05:54.160 --> 00:06:01.110
than platinum, but again it’s in the same
group in the periodic table as platinum. But

00:06:01.110 --> 00:06:09.680
it is used quite a lot in catalysis, and there
was a belief that perhaps palladium could

00:06:09.680 --> 00:06:18.400
catalyse nuclear fusion, cause deuterium atoms
to fuse together to produce energy. There

00:06:18.400 --> 00:06:27.050
was a lot of excitement in the late 1980s
but now most people feel that those experiments

00:06:27.050 --> 00:06:35.220
actually were not very reproducible and something
else was happening. This one is molybdenum.

00:06:35.220 --> 00:06:41.020
Molybdenum is particularly interesting as
it’s one of the few heavy elements which

00:06:41.020 --> 00:06:49.550
appears in enzymes.
Then round here we have tantalum which is

00:06:49.550 --> 00:06:59.760
the metal that is particularly used in so
called electrical capacitors. These are tiny

00:06:59.760 --> 00:07:06.820
components in electronic circuits which can
store electrical charge for a short time.

00:07:06.820 --> 00:07:13.520
They are a vital component in making mobile
telephones for example, without tantalum you

00:07:13.520 --> 00:07:19.580
couldn’t have mobile telephones. That’s
why people are beginning to recycle mobile

00:07:19.580 --> 00:07:25.500
telephones so they can recover some of these
metals. And finally we come down to this element

00:07:25.500 --> 00:07:35.840
here which is rhodium. Rhodium is one of the
best metals for catalysing chemical reactions.

00:07:35.840 --> 00:07:39.220
You haven’t seen it before, now you seen
it with your own eyes what do you think of

00:07:39.220 --> 00:07:42.710
it?
Well I don’t think it’s enormously beautiful

00:07:42.710 --> 00:07:46.150
but it would be really nice to have in my
office to teach students about the different

00:07:46.150 --> 00:07:50.230
metals.
Somebody told me just here, as we were coming

00:07:50.230 --> 00:07:58.361
down the stairs that in fact, when this was
made, about 60 years ago, some of these metals

00:07:58.361 --> 00:08:04.220
bars were the first time that that particular
metal had ever been made into a bar, so this

00:08:04.220 --> 00:08:11.140
is really quite a metallurgical achievement.
And presumably the people who made it learnt

00:08:11.140 --> 00:08:14.430
quite a lot about processing some of these
metals.

00:08:14.430 --> 00:08:18.140
You know what anyone who sees this, who isn’t
a chemist, and gets excited by metals are

00:08:18.140 --> 00:08:23.140
going to have one question, what’s it worth?
I’ve no idea, we’ll have to ask the guys

00:08:23.140 --> 00:08:28.540
from the Royal Society of Chemistry, but I
think it will be worth quite a lot because

00:08:28.540 --> 00:08:34.310
of the metals themselves, but it’s probably
the labour in making this might be quite as

00:08:34.310 --> 00:08:41.419
great as the value of the metals themselves.
The person who wears it is the current President

00:08:41.419 --> 00:08:47.130
of the Royal Society of Chemistry. At the
moment the President of the Royal Society

00:08:47.130 --> 00:08:52.580
of Chemistry is one of my colleagues from
Nottingham, Dave Garner. And it’s rather

00:08:52.580 --> 00:08:58.930
nice he’s a specialist in the chemistry
of molybdenum so when he goes round he will

00:08:58.930 --> 00:09:05.500
be wearing a thing with his favourite element
there. So I think most of the presidents will

00:09:05.500 --> 00:09:09.860
be able to find one of their favourite elements
there, and those that are organic chemists

00:09:09.860 --> 00:09:15.780
of course will have benzene in the middle.
And for the physical chemists who are closer

00:09:15.780 --> 00:09:20.090
to physics they probably like it because they
can look at the properties of the different

00:09:20.090 --> 00:09:22.170
metals.
You happy with all that?

00:09:22.170 --> 00:09:24.160
Yes I think we’d better put it back again
now.

00:09:24.160 --> 00:09:27.390
Okay I’ll leave you in the vault, I’ll
see you later man.

00:09:27.390 --> 00:09:31.080
Bye
Ha ha, okay, bye-bye. Let’s lock him in.

00:09:31.080 --> 00:09:33.450
No we better not do that.

